hi there it's kevin van or standing in  for reviewer Lafe johnson to tell you  about the open beta for hearthstone  hearthstone is the kind of game that  leaves you eager for another round this  is Magic the Gathering stripped of all  its clutter and it thrives on one on one  15-minute card battles that will likely  occupy scores of lunch breaks for years  to come hearthstone step isn't  immediately apparent in the compact 30  card deck but then few developers are so  adept at stripping complex concepts down  to their essentials as blizzard and in a  characteristic Blizzard touch not all  the fun comes from the play the most  powerful cards thump bond the playing  field with the force of rhinos and  hearthstone never eases back on the  atmosphere you get some idea of what  you're up against once hearthstone boots  you out of the short tutorial campaign  with the starter mage deck and into the  larger world to unlock the other classes  you have to fight the warriors that use  them whether they be AI or random  players unlocking all the classes  involves a zigzag of clicking on  different AI decks and testing the  waters until you find a deck you feel  comfortable with and then come the  pressures of deck building luckily  hearthstone makes this process  incredibly fun the game periodically  tosses new cards your way and in the  custom deck panel the suggest a card  feature helps make sure you have at  least a fighting chance against other  players you can fork out cash for  additional booster packs though you  probably won't feel the need to pay real  money in the early hours at higher  levels however good cards get so  numerous that hearthstone starts feeling  more like roulette than strategy  thankfully Blizzard provides an  alternative to any resulting frustration  that's the arena mode which requires  either cash or gold to play it's here  that hearthstone pushes your deck  building skills to their limits it  presents you with three random decks one  of which you need to whittle down to 30  cards to stay in the arena mode you have  to keep winning there's little doubt  that luck plays in  victory but achieving a victory delivers  the satisfaction that you won the match  on your own hearthstone is off to a good  start and its popularity will further  explode once it makes the leap to  tablets barring a failure of cataclysmic  proportions this is one open beta that  should serve as a model for all others  you
 

